# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CyAssure 360 — Docker environment template.
# Copy to .env, fill in the values, then: docker compose up -d
# Never commit the filled-in .env — it holds real secrets.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Required ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-strong-random-value
# Roadmap item #34 Phase 2 — a genuinely separate secret from
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD above, not a reuse. POSTGRES_PASSWORD authenticates
# corruser, a Postgres superuser used only once per container boot (see
# backend/core/db_least_privilege.py) to create/maintain the actual
# connection role, cyassure_app — which uses THIS password for every real
# request the app ever serves. Keeping them separate means a leak of the
# credential the app actually uses on every request never exposes the
# superuser credential too, and vice versa.
CYASSURE_APP_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-cyassure-app-random-value
SECRET_KEY=change-me-to-a-long-random-value
# ClickHouse's official image disables ALL network access for the default
# user if this is left empty — it does not mean "no password required" the
# way an unset Redis/Postgres password might. Required, not optional. Part
# of CyDataLake (see docs/CYDATALAKE_BUILDOUT_PLAN.md).
CYDATALAKE_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-clickhouse-random-value

# ── CyDataLake raw-log capture — ON by default for new installs (see
# docs/CYDATALAKE_BUILDOUT_PLAN.md). Every EDR, ASM, CyCollector, and SIEM
# connector event — including anything filtered out before it became a
# correlated alert — is captured into the bundled Redpanda/ClickHouse stack
# and queryable under CyDataLake in the sidebar (Settings → RBAC: admin and
# analyst roles only). Single switch, CYDATALAKE_ENABLED, for the whole
# pipeline. Manage this via Settings → CyDataLake in the UI, not by
# hand-editing this file — that toggle also stops/starts the
# redpanda/clickhouse/ingest-worker containers (via the optional self-update
# sidecar below) instead of just leaving them idling at baseline resource
# cost. Existing deployments upgrading from an older release: this only
# affects NEW installs that copy this template — your existing .env is
# untouched.
CYDATALAKE_ENABLED=true

# ── Optional: CyDataLake cold-storage retention (ingest-worker's daily
# export_and_purge_older_than() job — Parquet-archives then purges ClickHouse
# rows older than this many days). Defaults match the bundled
# cy360_cydatalake_archive volume — only set these if you want a
# non-default retention window.
# CYDATALAKE_COLD_STORAGE_DAYS=90
# CYDATALAKE_COLD_STORAGE_DIR=/var/lib/cyassure/cydatalake-archive

# ── Host port the app is reachable on (http://localhost:<APP_PORT>) ────────
APP_PORT=8080

# ── Which image tag to run — pin to a specific release, e.g. 1.2.3 ─────────
CYASSURE_VERSION=latest

# ── Optional: auto-derived as https://<BASE_DOMAIN> once you set BASE_DOMAIN
# below (or via Settings > Organization Domain / the Setup Wizard) — you
# don't need to set these yourself for the common case. Only set them if the
# public URL differs from BASE_DOMAIN alone, e.g. a non-standard reverse-
# proxy port.
# FRONTEND_URL=https://soc.yourcompany.com
# BASE_URL=https://soc.yourcompany.com

# ── Organization domain (recommended: set via the in-app Setup Wizard or
# Settings > Organization Domain instead of hand-editing this file — that UI
# validates the value and warns you about the consequences of changing it
# later). BASE_DOMAIN drives cookies/OAuth-SSO/MCP URLs. EXPOSURE_TARGET_DOMAIN
# is what the recurring External Exposure scan monitors — leave it unset to
# just inherit BASE_DOMAIN (the common case); only set it separately if you're
# monitoring a different organization's domain than the one this platform is
# hosted under.
# BASE_DOMAIN=yourcompany.com
# EXPOSURE_TARGET_DOMAIN=

# ── IAP Gateway (oauth2-proxy) — single OIDC login in front of the WHOLE app.
# Automatic, not a setting: OAUTH2PROXY_SECRET/OAUTH2PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET are
# generated for every install, the oauth2-proxy container always starts once
# they exist, and cyassure-setup.sh's "HOST VHOST + TLS PROVISIONING" step
# creates the host nginx vhost for BASE_DOMAIN itself if none exists yet
# (--tls-mode picks how the certificate is obtained — see DOCKER_DEPLOYMENT.md
# Section 3), already wired straight to it. If a vhost already exists (hand-
# written, or from an earlier install), it's automatically wired into that
# vhost's traffic path instead — on every Settings > Update/Upgrade click, and
# on every cyassure-setup.sh --update/--full run. No manual step in the common
# case. If you're fronting this app with something other than host nginx
# (Cloudflare Tunnel, another LB, etc.) — --tls-mode=none — point IT at
# 127.0.0.1:4180 yourself instead of the app port directly — see
# updater/server.py's _wire_iap_gateway_vhost().

# ── Self-update from the UI (ON by default) ─────────────────────────────────
# Lets the "Update"/"Upgrade" buttons in Settings, and the Setup Wizard's
# "Run cyassure-setup.sh Now" hardening option, actually act on this host
# instead of just showing you the commands to run yourself. ON by default —
# see DOCKER_DEPLOYMENT.md "Self-update" for the full tradeoff before you rely
# on it: the updater sidecar runs `privileged: true` + `pid: host` with a
# Docker-socket mount, which is genuine host-root-equivalent code execution,
# not just container-scoped access. If you don't want any container on this
# host capable of that, comment out COMPOSE_PROFILES below — every UI screen
# that would otherwise use it falls back to showing you the exact command to
# run yourself instead of failing silently.
#
# cyassure-setup.sh's "DOCKER APPLICATION" step already fills in GH_TOKEN and
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_DIR below for you. GH_TOKEN is also what
# /api/system/latest-version uses to check GitHub for a newer release, even
# if you disable self-update itself.
GH_TOKEN=
COMPOSE_PROJECT_DIR=
# Pins the compose project name so the updater sidecar (which always sees its
# bind-mounted project dir as the fixed path /project, not this directory's
# real name) recreates THIS stack instead of accidentally starting a second,
# separately-named one. cyassure-setup.sh sets this to your install
# directory's name automatically. Deploying manually instead? Set it to match whatever
# `docker compose ls` already shows for this stack BEFORE you ever activate
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=selfupdate — changing it after the fact makes Compose think
# this is a brand new project (new volumes, possible orphaned old containers).
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=
UPDATER_TOKEN=change-me-to-a-random-value
COMPOSE_PROFILES=selfupdate

# ── Optional integrations — uncomment and fill in what you use ─────────────
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=

# ── OpenVAS/GVM vulnerability assessment (OFF by default) ───────────────────
# Only reached for Deep-tier ASM scans (Settings > Scan Operations), and only
# if the separate, opt-in docker-compose.gvm.yml stack is also running (see
# its header comment — a genuinely heavy addition, real active network vuln
# scanning, not something to enable casually). Leave ENABLE_OPENVAS=false
# (or unset) unless you've deliberately stood up that stack.
# ENABLE_OPENVAS=true
# GVM_USER=admin
# GVM_PASSWORD=change-me

# ── Local LLM (Ollama) — dedicated hardware, OFF/unset by default ──────────
# CyMind's native AI Assistant (chat, RAG embeddings, smart routing —
# cy_mind/services/router_engine.py + llm_service.py) can dispatch to a
# self-hosted Ollama instance instead of (or alongside) an external provider.
# There is deliberately NO Ollama container in docker-compose.yml — LLM
# inference is GPU/RAM-hungry and belongs on its own dedicated box, never
# packed onto the same host as the rest of this stack, and never added as a
# compose service here. Stand up Ollama yourself (bare metal, a VM, or its
# own container elsewhere) on hardware sized for the models you intend to
# run, then point this instance at it:
#   OLLAMA_URL        — single endpoint, e.g. http://10.0.1.50:11434
#                        (unset default is http://127.0.0.1:11434, which
#                        inside the backend container never resolves to
#                        anything — you must set this explicitly)
#   OLLAMA_ENDPOINTS   — optional comma-separated list for multi-endpoint
#                        failover, e.g. http://gpu1:11434,http://gpu2:11434
#                        (health-checked at request time — router_engine.py's
#                        get_best_ollama_endpoint(); takes priority over
#                        OLLAMA_URL when set)
# Left commented out below deliberately (see docker-compose.yml's backend
# service comment) — an explicit-empty-string env var would permanently hide
# router_engine.py's own 127.0.0.1 fallback, so these are only ever passed
# into the container when you actually uncomment and set them here.
# OLLAMA_URL=http://your-dedicated-ollama-host:11434
# OLLAMA_ENDPOINTS=http://gpu1.internal:11434,http://gpu2.internal:11434

# ── Internal AI Gateway route — cysiemstack-engine's own AI calls ──────────
# The correlation engine runs in a separate container/image from the main
# backend (Dockerfile.engine, no cy_mind package) — its "cymind" AI provider
# option and its incident-episodic-memory storage
# (cysiemstack/correlation_engine/ai_router.py, llm_enricher.py) reach the
# native cy_mind LLM pipeline over HTTP instead of an in-process import, via
# the same Bearer-key Gateway route external Tier-1 integrations use
# (POST /api/ai-security/gateway/chat, /gateway/memory). Only needed if you
# select "cymind" as the AI provider in Settings -> AI Config; unset means
# that provider option falls back to reporting "not configured", every other
# provider option is unaffected.
#   CY360_INTERNAL_AI_URL — defaults to http://backend:5252 (the backend
#                            service's own name on this compose network);
#                            only set this if you've changed that.
#   CY360_INTERNAL_AI_KEY — a cymk_... key, mint one in the portal under
#                            Enterprise Add-ons -> AI Gateway -> Gateway Keys.
# CY360_INTERNAL_AI_URL=http://backend:5252
# CY360_INTERNAL_AI_KEY=cymk_...

# ── Disaster recovery — offsite backup (OFF by default) ─────────────────────
# Pushes every backup (manual or the scheduled "Automated Backup" task) to an
# S3-compatible bucket in addition to the local /opt/cyassure/backups copy —
# see docs/DISASTER_RECOVERY_RUNBOOK.md for the restore procedure and stated
# RTO/RPO. Works with real AWS S3 or any S3-compatible target (MinIO, Wasabi,
# Backblaze B2, Ceph RGW, DigitalOcean Spaces...) — set BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT_URL
# for anything other than AWS. Leave BACKUP_S3_BUCKET unset to keep
# local-only backups (unchanged default behavior). Credentials here can
# instead be centrally managed via your secrets backend (Infisical/Azure KV/
# HashiCorp Vault — see core/kv_secrets.py's BACKUP_KV_MAP) rather than set
# in this file.
# BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=cyassure-backups-yourcompany
# BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT_URL=
# BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=cyassure-backups

# ── Fleet monitor heartbeat (ON by default) ─────────────────────────────────
# Sends a periodic, metadata-only heartbeat — instance identity, uptime,
# platform version, and license/edition status — to CyAdmin's fleet-monitoring
# endpoint, so it can show whether this instance is active, how long it's been
# up, and which license edition/limits it's running under. No logs, no scan
# results, no customer data of any kind are ever sent. Unauthenticated by
# design (out-of-band signal only) — no enrollment key or token needed. See
# docs/LICENSE_AND_FLEET_HEARTBEAT_SPEC.md for the exact payload/endpoint.
# Defaults to the same host as MARKETPLACE_CATALOG_URL (core/config.py) —
# only set FLEET_MONITOR_URL below if fleet monitoring is ever split onto a
# different host. Set FLEET_HEARTBEAT_ENABLED=false to opt out entirely.
FLEET_HEARTBEAT_ENABLED=true
# FLEET_MONITOR_URL=
# Written automatically on first heartbeat — do not set by hand:
FLEET_INSTANCE_ID=
